WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:57.000
Well, I thought my car had been stolen, which I was like, does that even happen? I guess it does, but, you know, my car is really old. It's like 2011 Mazda 6, so I really didn't know why anyone would want to take that car. This is Kelly Prime. We work together at 99PI and she lives in Brooklyn. And not too long ago, she had an experience that shook her. She and her friends had been out for the day in Coney Island. and they were stopping to get some takeout on the way home. They were looking for a place to park, not too far from the restaurant. And, you know, this is Brooklyn, so there's not a lot of parking available, and all the street parking looked like it was filled up. But on the right-hand side, we come upon this brightly lit, beautiful, empty parking lot in front of a 7-Eleven. Perfect, like irresistible, it sounds like.
嗯，我还以为我的车被偷了，当时我就想，这种事真的会发生吗？我想会的吧，但你知道，我的车真的很旧。那是2011年的马自达6，所以我是真不知道会有人想要偷那辆车。这是凯莉·普莱姆（Kelly Prime）。我们在99PI一起工作，她住在布鲁克林。不久前，她经历了一件让她心有余悸的事。她和朋友们那天去了康尼岛（Coney Island）玩。他们打算在回家的路上顺便买点外卖。他们在离餐厅不太远的地方找地方停车。而且，你知道的，这里是布鲁克林，所以没有多少空余的停车位，而且所有街边车位看起来都停满了。但在右手边，我们遇到了一家7-Eleven前面灯火通明、非常漂亮、空无一人的停车场。完美，听起来就像无法抗拒一样。

2
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:29.000
Irresistible, an oasis. This allure, some might say. So we parked in the parking lot, pulled up, only car there. What a miracle. And just ran out into the supermarket and spent probably like, you know, 15 minutes grabbing our stuff. They came back out, went to the 7-Eleven parking lot, and the car wasn't there. Disappeared. The car was gone. And I was sort of in denial for a second.
无法抗拒，简直就是一片绿洲。有人可能会说是这种诱惑。所以我们把车停在了停车场，开了进去，那是里面唯一的一辆车。简直是个奇迹。然后我们小跑着冲进超市，大概花了，你知道的，15分钟左右的时间拿了我们的东西。他们出来后，回到了7-Eleven的停车场，车却不在了。消失了。车不见了。我有一会儿简直不敢相信。

3
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:59.000
I was like, well, surely. Like, I just, I'm not that great at remembering where I parked anyway, but it's not a big lot, and it's super brightly lit, and there's no cars there. So she and her friends went into the 7-Eleven to ask if anyone had seen anything. We go to the register, and we're like, I think our car just got stolen. Did you see anyone steal a car? And the guy was like, no, it didn't get stolen, got towed. And this guy is just acting like,
我想，呃，这怎么可能。我是说，我本来就不太擅长记住自己把车停在哪儿，但这停车场不大，而且灯光超级亮，里面一辆车也没有。于是她和朋友们走进7-Eleven，问有没有人看到什么。我们走到收银台前，说：“我觉得我们的车刚刚被偷了。你们看到有人偷车吗？”那个男的说：“没有，没被偷，被拖走了。”这人的态度就好像……

4
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Like he's never heard something more boring in his life. And he was like, yeah, like 20 cars get towed a day. There's a sign out there called the number. Back out in the parking lot, they did find signs warning that unauthorized cars would be towed. One was in Cyrillic. One was covered in graffiti. And another was tucked away in a far corner of the lot. Kelly had the idea to pull up the Google reviews for the 7-Eleven.
就好像他这辈子从来没听过比这更无聊的事一样。然后他说：“对啊，一天大概有20辆车被拖走。外面有标牌，上面有电话号码。”回到停车场后，他们确实找到了警告未经授权的车辆会被拖走的标牌。其中一个是西里尔字母的。一个被涂鸦覆盖了。另一个则藏在停车场的一个角落里。凯莉想到了去查一下这家7-Eleven的谷歌评价。

5
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:48.000
And they found dozens of people telling pretty much. the same story. Says, I was their customer for many years, gave a great business for all those years, till my car was towed less than six minutes absence. If you read the other one-star review, you'll understand what's going on. This store is running a scam. We bought drinks in the store, and someone called the towing. Never buy anything from this store.
然后他们发现了几十个人的评价，说的几乎都是同一件事。上面写着：“我当了他们多年的顾客，这么多年来为他们贡献了很好的生意，直到我的车在不到六分钟的离开时间内被拖走。”如果你看了另一条一星差评，你就会明白是怎么回事了。这家店在搞诈骗。我们在店里买了饮料，然后有人打电话叫了拖车。千万不要在这家店买任何东西。

6
00:02:48.000 --> 00:03:21.000
Call 3-1-1. Terrible and unethical. Take your car hostage and get $150 from you. I'm Delaney Hall, and this is service request from 99% Invisible. and campside media. We take your burning questions about infrastructure, and we investigate. Kelly eventually got her car back that night, and we will get into that. But the whole experience left her with a question that she could not shake.
打311举报吧。太可怕了，太不道德了。把你的车当人质扣押，然后从你那儿敲诈150美元。我是德莱尼·霍尔（Delaney Hall），这是来自《99%无形》（99% Invisible）和Campside Media的“服务请求”（Service Request）栏目。我们收集你们对基础设施的迫切疑问，并进行调查。凯莉那天晚上最终拿回了她的车，我们稍后会详细讲到。但整个经历给她留下的一个疑问让她挥之不去。

7
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Or really, a whole bunch of questions. How is it that you can just park your car? 15 minutes later, it's gone. I don't know who's in charge of regulating this. I don't know. who empowers these tow truck guys under whose authority are they working? It just feels like this black box of mystery that's affecting clearly a lot of people
或者确切地说，是一连串的问题。怎么就能随随便便把车停好？15分钟后，车就没了。我不知道是谁负责监管这事。我不知道到底是谁赋予了这些拖车司机权力，他们是在谁的授权下工作的？这感觉就像是一个黑箱谜团，显然对很多人造成了影响

8
00:03:44.000 --> 00:04:13.000
if the Google reviews are to be believed. Today, the black box of mystery of towing and impoundment. Who decides when your car gets taken? Who decides where it goes? And when things go wrong, which it turns out, they do a lot. Why is it so hard to fix? When we started trying to find the answer to Kelly's question, I learned the term that describes this kind of practice. It's called predatory towing.
如果谷歌评价可信的话。今天，我们就来揭开拖车和扣车黑箱背后的谜团。谁来决定你的车什么时候被拖走？谁来决定它被拖到哪里？当事情出了岔子——事实证明，这种情况并不少见——为什么修复起来这么难？当我们开始试图寻找凯莉的问题的答案时，我学到了一个用来描述这种做法的术语。这叫掠夺性拖车（predatory towing）。

9
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:41.000
And it describes towers who actively hunt for cars to pick up and then demand inflated fees when owners try to get them back. Predatory towing happens everywhere. But in some places, it happens a lot. Detroit, Michigan has a well-documented and long-running predatory towing problem. And so that makes it a good case study for us. I mean, it's a big business. It's a multi-million dollar business, and they make a lot of money doing this. Yeah.
它指的是那些疯狂搜寻车辆进行拖曳，然后在车主试图赎回车辆时索取虚高费用的拖车公司。掠夺性拖车无处不在。但在某些地方，这种情况非常多。密歇根州底特律市长期以来一直存在严重的、有据可查的掠夺性拖车问题。所以这为我们提供了一个很好的案例研究。我的意思是，这是一门大生意。这是一门产值达数百万美元的生意，他们靠这个赚了很多钱。是的。

10
00:04:41.000 --> 00:05:25.000
So that's why they do it. This is Tom Berry. He spent 35 years as a police officer in Detroit, and he retired as a lieutenant about a decade ago. Since then, he's worked as a fraud investigator for insurance companies. And he has seen the towing industry from basically every angle. He's been a cop who called tow trucks, and he's been an investigator who's uncovered how corrupt towing companies actually operate. I'm curious, can you help us understand, in an ideal world, how is towing supposed to be a public good? What kind of service do toowers provide when they're working well?
所以这就是他们这么做的原因。这是汤姆·巴里（Tom Berry）。他在底特律当了35年警察，大约十年前以中尉军衔退休。从那以后，他一直担任保险公司的欺诈调查员。他基本上从各个角度见证了拖车行业。他当过需要呼叫拖车的警察，也当过调查员，揭露过腐败的拖车公司究竟是如何运作的。我很好奇，您能帮我们理解一下，在理想世界中，拖车本该是如何发挥公共福利作用的吗？当拖车公司运作良好时，它们提供的是什么样的服务？

11
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:58.000
If it's working good, which we all like to see, you live on Elm Avenue and you come out one day and you can't get out of your driveway because somebody's parked there. So, of course, you call the police because you've got to go to work. And the police call the tow truck and the tow truck comes out and moves that car out of your way so you can go about your business. That's a good deal. Also, we have accidents. Once they take care of the injured, we need to remove those cars from the road. The tow companies take them to a tow yard. so it can be good.
如果运作良好的话——这是我们都乐见其成的——你住在埃尔姆大道（Elm Avenue），有一天你出门，却发现车开不出车库了，因为有人把车停在那儿。所以，当然了，你得报警，因为你要去上班。然后警察叫来拖车，拖车赶到现场把车移开，这样你就可以去忙你的正事了。这很合理。还有，我们会遇到交通事故。一旦伤者得到妥善处理，我们就需要把这些车从路上移走。拖车公司把它们拉到停车场，所以这可以是件好事。

12
00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:22.000
In many cities, when there's a crash that needs to be cleared or a vehicle that's been abandoned on the street, police will call a private towing company to call it away. That's how it works in Detroit, too. Tom says that when he was a police officer, the department gave him a list of the tow companies that he was allowed to call. These were companies that had a contract with the police
在许多城市，当发生需要清理的撞车事故或有车辆被遗弃在街上时，警察会打电话给私营拖车公司将其拖走。底特律也是这样运作的。汤姆说，当他还是个警察时，警局会给他一份允许呼叫的拖车公司名单。这些公司都是与警方签有合同的公司

13
00:06:22.000 --> 00:07:01.000
to cover a certain area of the city. And most officers would go down the approved list calling companies until one was available to send a tow truck. But years later, as a lieutenant, Tom discovered that not all police officers were doing it that way. They were using a certain toer. They weren't calling the scheduled toers, the toers on the list. They were calling their friend. Some cops were calling tow companies they had a relationship with. And many of those companies were inflating their fees way beyond what a typical tow would cost.
负责城市的一定区域。大多数警员会顺着获批的名单打电话，直到有一家公司有空派拖车过来。但几年后，作为中尉的汤姆发现，并非所有的警员都按规矩办事。他们一直在使用某家特定的拖车公司。他们没有叫排班表上的拖车，也没有叫名单上的拖车。他们在叫他们的朋友。有些警察在呼叫他们有关系的拖车公司。其中许多公司大大抬高了收费，远超正常拖车应有的成本。

14
00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:34.000
Then they'd pay kickbacks to the police officers who sent the business their way. And the corruption kind of swelled. It just keeps surmounting and eventually, you know, it comes crashing down. That's what happened here in Detroit. Eventually, there was a federal investigation into the corruption that Tom is talking about. And some cops went to jail. But that wasn't the end of towing problems in the city. In fact, Tom says some of the most problematic towing doesn't even involve the police.
然后，他们会把回扣给那些把生意介绍给他们的警察。这种腐败就像滚雪球一样不断膨胀。它一直在加剧，最终，你知道的，彻底崩盘了。底特律发生的就是这种情况。最终，联邦对汤姆所说的这种腐败行为展开了调查。一些警察锒铛入狱。但这并不是这座城市拖车问题的终结。事实上，汤姆说，一些问题最大的拖车甚至不涉及警方。

15
00:07:34.000 --> 00:08:09.000
It involves situations like Kellys, where people get towed for parking in a private lot. I'm curious if, kind of zooming out, you can walk me through how a predatory towing operation works. Like, what does a typical day look like? what are the most common schemes that you see? Well, I think that most toors are basically honest. First, I want to give them a little accolade. So what happens? Like your friend there at the 7-Eleven.
它涉及的是像凯莉那样的遭遇，人们因为把车停在私人停车场而被拖走。我很好奇，如果稍微跳出宏观视角，您能向我介绍一下掠夺性拖车是怎么运作的吗？比如，典型的一天是什么样的？您见过的最常见的套路有哪些？嗯，我认为大多数拖车司机基本上都是诚实的。首先，我想对他们大加赞赏一番。那么情况是怎样的呢？就像你那位在7-Eleven的朋友一样。

16
00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:49.000
So it's illegal to park and private property and go to the doctor's office, let's say, or go do your shopping. So if you own a business in your city, you know, McDonald's or Burger King or whatever, and you have private citizens that aren't patronizing your business tying up your lot, and they keep coming in there, and you're losing customers because your lot's full of people going to shop at Macy's, then you're mad. So you, the business owner, can contact, you know, Joe's towing and say, hey, I got a red Chevy out in my lot. Can you come and get it?
所以，把车停在私人物业然后去看医生，比方说，或者去购物，这是违法的。因此，如果你在城里拥有一家企业，你知道的，麦当劳、汉堡王之类的，而有一些不是来你店里消费的普通市民占用了你的车位，他们一直把车停在那儿，导致你因为停车场停满了去梅西百货（Macy's）购物的人而失去顾客，那你就会很生气。所以你，作为企业老板，可以联系，你知道的，乔伊拖车（Joe's towing），说：“嘿，我的停车场里停了一辆红色的雪佛兰。你们能来把它拖走吗？”

17
00:08:49.000 --> 00:09:20.000
It's been here for a half hour. This practice makes sense. A business needs to keep its lot clear for actual customers. And that's what happened to Kelly. She parked at a 7-Eleven, walked to a neighboring restaurant, and then came back to find her car gone. She technically broke the rules, and the tow company had the right to take her car. But there are sketchier tactics that some toowers use to maximize the number of cars they can grab. For instance, spotters.
它已经停了半个小时了。这种做法是合乎情理的。商家需要保持停车场畅通，以便真正有需求的顾客使用。凯莉就是遭遇了这种情况。她把车停在7-Eleven，步行到隔壁餐厅，回来后发现车没了。严格来说她违反了规定，拖车公司有权把车拖走。但有些拖车公司会使用一些更鬼祟的战术，来尽量多拖走一些车。例如，使用“线人”（spotters）。

18
00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:46.000
Spotters are paid citizens that live in that area. I approach them and say, hey, I'm the owner of Joe's towing. And I know that your apartment overlooks the Burger King lot over here. So we meet. It's okay, here's what I want you to do. I want you to watch this lot. And if you see somebody go into that lot and not use the business, you call me. And so I'll be right there.
线人是住在当地并获得报酬的居民。我找到他们然后说：“嘿，我是乔伊拖车的老板。我知道你的公寓正对着这边的汉堡王停车场。所以我们见个面。行了，这是我希望你做的。我希望你帮我盯着这个停车场。如果你看到有人把车停进停车场却不是去消费的，你就给我打电话。这样我就会立刻赶到。”

19
00:09:46.000 --> 00:10:12.000
And if I get the tow job, you get 50 or 100 bucks. So tow truck makes cash. People make 50 bucks, no taxes. Everybody's happy except the person that parked there. Except the person who parked there. So this is interesting. Some tow companies, they basically recruit a neighborhood network of eyes on the ground, of spies. That's a good way to put it.
“如果我接成了这单拖车生意，你就能拿50或100块钱。”所以拖车司机赚了现金。人们赚了50块钱，不用交税。除了把车停在那儿的那个人，每个人都很高兴。除了那个把车停在那儿的人。所以这很有意思。有些拖车公司，他们基本上在社区里招募了一张地面眼线网，也就是间谍网。这么说倒也贴切。

20
00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:40.000
A neighborhood network. Excellent. People who are just keeping an eye on these various parking lots. And when they see someone park there and not use the business, they just give the tow truck driver a call and they get a kickback. Exactly. In some cities, it's illegal for tow companies to use spotters. But not in New York City or in Detroit. One tow company there became so notorious for using spotters, they felt the need to put out this little explainer video on their Facebook page.
一张社区眼线网。太妙了。这些人只是盯着这些不同的停车场。当他们看到有人把车停在那儿却没有消费时，就会给拖车司机打个电话，然后拿回扣。没错。在某些城市，拖车公司使用线人是违法的。但在纽约市或底特律并非如此。当地有一家拖车公司因为使用线人而变得臭名昭著，以至于他们觉得有必要在自己的Facebook主页上发布这段简短的科普视频。

21
00:10:40.000 --> 00:11:29.000
A lot of people think a spotter's illegal, but it's not. There's nothing illegal about it. They're just maintaining parking spaces for the property owner. After the break, we'll talk with a driver who used to work for that notorious Detroit tow company. Well, I guess to start, could you just introduce yourself, like, your name and where you are and what you're doing? My name is Shane Nation. I'm in Detroit, Michigan, and I currently tow, and I've been in the industry for about five years. When I spoke with Shane, he was in his toe. truck between calls on his lunch break. As far as I can tell, there aren't a lot of tow truck
很多人觉得线人是违法的，但其实并不是。这没有任何违法的。他们只是在为业主维护停车位。休息过后，我们将与一位曾为那家臭名昭泽的底特律拖车公司工作的司机聊聊。嗯，我想先请您做个自我介绍，比如您的名字、您在哪里以及您在做什么？我叫沙恩·内森（Shane Nation）。我在密歇根州底特律，我目前从事拖车工作，在这个行业大约有五年了。我和沙恩交谈时，他正坐在午休期间两单任务之间的拖车上。据我所知，并没有多少拖车

22
00:11:29.000 --> 00:12:17.000
drivers who are willing to talk with a journalist about the realities of the job, but Shane is one of them. He started in the industry when he was just 16 years old. Once I started like working in the industry, it's like a bug. It's very hard to go do something where you're not as free. Like go sit in an office or inside all day long. And it honestly, I, It's not boring. There's people that have been towing for 25 years and they'll tell you that they learn new stuff every day because it's just genuinely that type of job. So for people who are not familiar with towing, what is a typical day like in the life of a tow truck driver? You normally sit in the truck, you plan on your phone, do whatever to let time pass. And then you just wait for police calls to come in.
司机愿意跟记者谈论这份工作的现实情况，但沙恩就是其中之一。他16岁时就开始进入这个行业了。一旦我开始在这个行业工作，就像是上瘾了一样。你很难再去从事那种不够自由的工作。比如去办公室坐着，或者一整天都待在室内。老实说，我，这绝不无聊。有些人干了25年拖车，他们会告诉你他们每天都能学到新东西，因为这真的是那种类型的工作。所以对于不熟悉拖车的人来说，拖车司机的真实生活日常是什么样的？你通常坐在卡车里，玩手机打发时间，干点别的来消磨时间。然后你就是等警方电话打进来。

23
00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:40.000
Those could look like impounds, like somebody got arrested. and we would tow their car to impound lot. It could look like a car crash, and we have to be out there cleaning the scene on the highway or something. And it could be as simple as your mom broke down, and she just needs a tow to the mechanic shop and to get her car fixed. Have you towed your mom? Yeah, not my mother.
这些可能包括扣车，比如有人被逮捕了，我们就得把他们的车拖到扣车场。也可能是车祸，我们需要去高速公路或别的地方清理现场。或者也可能很简单，比如你妈妈的车抛锚了，她只需要把车拖到修理厂去修好。你拖过你妈妈的车吗？对，不是我妈。

24
